course: dictionary
lesson: 12000
title: Characteristically: just as usual
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Characteristically is a long word, but it is built from parts you may know. A characteristic is a typical feature, the thing that makes a person or thing recognisable. Add -ly and you get an adverb, a word that tells you how something is done. So characteristically means in the typical way, just as that person or thing usually does.
explain: «Characteristically, Grandma brought cake.» Ask yourself: what does Grandma usually do? If she always brings cake, then the word tells you this is what she always does, so it is no surprise. Try the swap: "Grandma brought cake, as she usually does." It still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Science texts use the word in the same way, but for features. «The oak leaves are characteristically lobed.» This tells you that lobed leaves are the typical feature of an oak, the sort of thing you look for to recognise one. Look for clue words such as typical, usual, or the feature being named.
explain: Related words help too. A characteristic is the typical feature itself, and typically means usually. If you know typically, you already know most of characteristically. When context is not enough, a dictionary settles it.
fact: Characteristically means in a way that is typical of a particular person or thing.
fact: «Characteristically» means as they usually do, or in a way that shows a special feature of something.
fact: A characteristic is a typical feature, such as a shape, a colour or a habit.
fact: «Characteristically» is an adverb: it tells you how something happens or is done.
fact: Related words: «characteristic» (a typical feature) and «typically» (usually).
exercise: «Characteristically, Ben laughed loudly at his own joke.» What does characteristically mean? | Quietly, so no one could hear | Late, after everyone else had laughed | *In a way that is typical of Ben | In a surprising way that nobody expected
exercise: What is a characteristic? | A place to sit down | A sudden loud noise | A kind of weather | *A typical feature of something
exercise: Which word in this sentence means as it usually does? «Characteristically, the robin sang from the top of the fence.» | fence | sang | *Characteristically | robin
test: «Characteristically, the cat curled up on the warm mat.» What does this tell you? | *The cat did what it usually does | The cat was cross and hissing | The cat was lost and looking for home | The cat was visiting a new house
test: Which word means a typical feature of a plant or an animal? | *characteristic | puzzle | puddle | sunlight
exam: Ayesha says characteristically means sometimes. Is she right? | Yes, it means very quickly | Yes, it means now and then | No, it means very loudly | *No, it means in the usual, typical way
